  "machineKind": "laser",
  "metadata": {
    "job": "Round bar center finder",
    "revision": "B",
    "notes": "Glue the fence under the plate, flush along the plate's two straight edges, so the lips hang down; pin the parts together through the four ⌀1.5 alignment holes (wire or brads) while the glue sets, then pull the pins. Set the plate on the end of the bar and push the corner against it: the lips' inside faces touch the bar, so its center is on the slot's line. Scribe along the slot, move the tool round the bar, scribe again; the lines cross at the center. The mill file's two parts, cut on a laser: same variables, same sketch. The Cut layer carries the beam recipe, so re-tuning it re-tunes every operation. The holes and the slot are cut first and the outlines last: once an outline is severed the part is loose on the bed. Power and speed are starting points for 3mm cast acrylic on a CO2 laser; test on offcuts. The beam has width, so the layer compensates by its kerf: the path runs half a kerf outside each outline and half a kerf inside the slot, which puts the lips' faces and the slot on the drawn lines."
  },
